lesson "What a derivative is" {
  course: "calculus"
  unit: "Derivatives & Rules"
  difficulty: "Intermediate"
  icon: "TrendingUp"
  summary: "A speedometer shows a speed at one instant, when no time passes. Shrink the gap, watch the average settle, and find out what that number really is."
  skills: ["derivative-meaning", "derivative-from-definition"]

  role gap = primary
  role distance = accent
  role speed = success

  slide "Warm up" {
    id: "d-check"
    beat: check
    > Quick one before we start.

    scene plane {
      x: [-0.3, 4.3]
      y: [-1.2, 7.4]
      grid
      axes
      line l = (0, -1) -> (4, 7) { color: primary, width: 3 }
      point a = (1, 1) { label: "(1, 1)" }
      point b = (3, 5) { label: "(3, 5)" }
    }

    numeric {
      ask "How steep is this line? Give its slope."
      skill: "slope-of-line"
      answer: 2
      wrong 4 "That's how far it climbs in total, but it takes 2 steps across to do it." -> "d-bridge-slope" retry
      wrong 0.5 "That's across over up. Slope is up over across." -> "d-bridge-slope" retry
      hint "How far up does it go, for how far across?"
      ! "Up 4 for 2 across is 2 up for every step across."
    }
  }

  slide "Slope, one step at a time" {
    id: "d-bridge-slope"
    beat: bridge
    hidden: true
    then: "d-bridge-slope-2"
    > Slope is how far a line goes [up]{distance} for every one step [across]{gap}. Drag the right-hand point and watch the steps.

    scene plane {
      x: [-0.3, 4.4]
      y: [-0.6, 14]
      grid
      axes
      param top = 5 { range: [1, 13], step: 1 }
      line l = (0, 1) -> (4, top) { color: neutral, width: 2 }
      for i in range(0, 4) {
        line f"run{i}" = (i, 1 + i*(top - 1)/4) -> (i + 1, 1 + i*(top - 1)/4) { color: gap, width: 3 }
        line f"up{i}" = (i + 1, 1 + i*(top - 1)/4) -> (i + 1, 1 + (i + 1)*(top - 1)/4) { color: distance, width: 3 }
      }
      point start = (0, 1)
      point end = (4, top) { drag: y -> top, snap: 1, color: distance }
      label at (0.15, 13) = "up per step across = ${(top - 1)/4}" { color: distance }
    }

    goal "Make a line that goes up 3 for every step across" {
      when: top == 13
      hints: ["There are 4 steps across. Each one has to climb 3.", "3 up on each of 4 steps is 12 up in total, starting from 1."]
      showme: { top: 13 }
    }
  }

  slide "Same idea, new line" {
    id: "d-bridge-slope-2"
    beat: bridge
    hidden: true
    > Now one you haven't seen.

    scene plane {
      x: [-0.3, 3.3]
      y: [-0.5, 8.5]
      grid
      axes
      line l = (0, 1) -> (2.4, 8.2) { color: primary, width: 3 }
      point a = (0, 1) { label: "(0, 1)" }
      point b = (2, 7) { label: "(2, 7)" }
    }

    numeric {
      ask "What's the slope of this line?"
      skill: "slope-of-line"
      answer: 3
      expect: (7 - 1) / (2 - 0)
      wrong 6 "That's the whole climb. Share it out over the steps across."
      hint "How far up from one point to the other, and how far across?"
    }
  }

  slide "The speedometer" {
    id: "d-hook"
    beat: hook
    > A speedometer says 60 km/h at exactly 3:00. But speed is distance over time, and in one instant no time passes. So what is it actually showing?

    quiz {
      ask "What is the speedometer showing?"
      skill: "derivative-meaning"
      * "Something real, but we need a new idea to say what"
      - "Nothing real, it's rounding" { why: "Hold that thought. In a minute you'll watch a number settle that isn't rounding anything." }
      - "The average over the last minute" { why: "Close, and keep that idea. A minute is a long time to a car that's speeding up, so we'll shrink it." }
      - "60 divided by 0" { why: "Dividing by 0 doesn't give a number, and the dial shows one. So something else is going on." }
      ! "There's no wrong bet here. Let's find out."
    }
  }

  slide "Average speed over one second" {
    id: "d-avg"
    beat: explore
    > Here's a car. After $t$ seconds it has gone $t^2$ metres. Between $t = 1$ and $t = 2$, what was its [average speed]{speed}?

    scene plane {
      x: [-0.2, 2.6]
      y: [-0.5, 6]
      grid
      axes
      curve f = x^2 { where: x >= 0, color: neutral, width: 3 }
      line run = (1, 1) -> (2, 1) { color: gap, width: 4 }
      line rise = (2, 1) -> (2, 4) { color: distance, width: 4 }
      point a = (1, 1) { label: "(1, 1)" }
      point b = (2, 4) { label: "(2, 4)" }
      label at (1.15, 0.55) = "gap = 1 second" { color: gap }
      label at (2.08, 2.5) = "distance covered = ?" { color: distance }
    }

    numeric {
      ask "Average speed between $t = 1$ and $t = 2$?"
      skill: "derivative-meaning"
      unit: "m/s"
      answer: 3
      expect: (2^2 - 1^2) / (2 - 1)
      wrong 4 "4 m is where the car is at $t = 2$, not how far it went in that second." -> "d-detour-distance" retry
      wrong 1 "1 m is where the car is at $t = 1$, not how far it went." -> "d-detour-distance" retry
      hint "Average speed is the [distance covered]{distance} divided by the [time it took]{gap}."
    }
  }

  slide "Shrink the gap" {
    id: "d-shrink"
    beat: explore
    > 3 m/s is the average over a whole second. But the car was speeding up the whole time. Shrink the [gap]{gap} and watch the [average]{speed}.

    scene plane {
      x: [-0.2, 2.4]
      y: [-0.5, 5.6]
      grid
      axes
      param h = 1 { range: [0.001, 1], step: 0.001, keep }
      curve f = x^2 { where: x >= 0, color: neutral, width: 3 }
      line run = (1, 1) -> (1 + h, 1) { color: gap, width: 4 }
      line rise = (1 + h, 1) -> (1 + h, (1 + h)^2) { color: distance, width: 4 }
      point a = (1, 1)
      point b = (1 + h, (1 + h)^2) { color: distance }
      label at (0.05, 5.2) = "gap = ${h} s" { color: gap }
      label at (0.05, 4.7) = "average speed over the gap = ${fixed(((1 + h)^2 - 1)/h, 3)} m/s" { color: speed }
      slider h { label: "gap" }
    }

    goal "Get the [gap]{gap} below 0.01 seconds" {
      when: h < 0.01
      hints: ["Keep your eye on the [average]{speed}, not the graph.", "Try gaps of 0.1, then 0.01, then 0.001. What stays the same?"]
      showme: { h: 0.001 }
      dur: 3500
    }

    numeric {
      ask "What number is the [average speed]{speed} settling on?"
      skill: "derivative-meaning"
      unit: "m/s"
      answer: 2
      wrong 3 "3 is the average over a whole second. Shrink the gap and it changes." -> "d-detour-big-gap" retry
      wrong 0 "The gap shrinks towards 0, but the average doesn't. Look at the readout." -> "d-detour-big-gap" retry
      wrong 2.001 "That's the average over your last gap. Shrink it more and it changes again. Where is it heading?"
      after: goals
    }
  }

  slide "What the speedometer shows" {
    id: "d-reveal"
    beat: reveal
    > You bet on "${answer("d-hook", "a guess")}". Here's what happened: the average didn't drop to 0 and didn't blow up. It settled on ${answer("d-shrink", "2")}. That's what a speedometer shows: the speed the average heads for as the gap shrinks.

    scene plane {
      x: [-0.2, 2.4]
      y: [-0.5, 5.6]
      grid
      axes
      param h = 0.1 { range: [0.0001, 1] }
      curve f = x^2 { where: x >= 0, color: neutral, width: 3 }
      line rise = (1 + h, 1) -> (1 + h, (1 + h)^2) { color: distance, width: 4 }
      point a = (1, 1)
      point b = (1 + h, (1 + h)^2) { color: distance }
      label at (0.05, 5.2) = "gap = ${h} s" { color: gap }
      label at (0.05, 4.7) = "average speed = ${fixed(((1 + h)^2 - 1)/h, 2)} m/s" { color: speed }
      step "The gap keeps shrinking..." { set: { h: 0.1 } }
      step "...and the average stops moving." { animate: { h: 0.0001 }, dur: 2500, ease: easeOut }
    }

    numeric {
      ask "Another car's average speed over gaps of 0.1, 0.01 and 0.001 seconds comes out as 5.3, 5.03 and 5.003 m/s. What does its speedometer show?"
      skill: "derivative-meaning"
      unit: "m/s"
      answer: 5
      wrong 5.3 "That's the average over the biggest gap, the least like one instant." -> "d-detour-big-gap" retry
      wrong 5.003 "That's still an average, over 0.001 s. Shrink the gap further and it moves again. Where is it heading?"
      after: 2
    }
  }

  slide "Why not a gap of 0?" {
    id: "d-trap"
    beat: trap
    > So why not skip the shrinking and set the [gap]{gap} to 0? Go on, drag it all the way.

    scene plane {
      x: [-0.2, 2.4]
      y: [-0.5, 5.6]
      grid
      axes
      param h = 0.5 { range: [0, 1], step: 0.01 }
      curve f = x^2 { where: x >= 0, color: neutral, width: 3 }
      line rise = (1 + h, 1) -> (1 + h, (1 + h)^2) { color: distance, width: 4 }
      point a = (1, 1)
      point b = (1 + h, (1 + h)^2) { color: distance }
      label at (0.05, 5.2) = "${fixed((1 + h)^2 - 1, 2)} metres in ${fixed(h, 2)} seconds" { color: distance }
      label at (0.05, 4.7) = "average speed = ${fixed(((1 + h)^2 - 1)/h, 2)} m/s" { color: speed }
      slider h { label: "gap" }
    }

    goal "Set the [gap]{gap} to 0" {
      when: h == 0
      hints: ["Drag the gap slider all the way to the left."]
      showme: { h: 0 }
      dur: 1500
    }

    quiz {
      ask "Why does a gap of 0 fail?"
      skill: "derivative-meaning"
      * "0 metres in 0 seconds is 0 divided by 0, which isn't a number"
      - "The car stops moving when the gap is 0" { why: "The car keeps going. It's our fraction that breaks, not the car." }
      - "It works, and the speed is 0" { why: "The readout says ?, not 0. No distance over no time isn't 0 m/s, it's nothing at all." }
      - "It works, and the speed is 2" { why: "2 is where the average heads, but at a gap of 0 the fraction is 0 over 0. You only get the 2 by watching it close in." }
      after: goals
      ! "Tempting, and it's exactly what people tried for centuries. We never let the gap reach 0. We watch where the average is heading."
    }
  }

  slide "The same thing as a picture" {
    id: "d-picture"
    beat: explore
    > Here's the same idea on the graph. The line through the two points has a slope, and that slope is the [average speed]{speed}.

    scene plane {
      x: [-0.2, 2.4]
      y: [-0.5, 5.6]
      grid
      axes
      param h = 1 { range: [0.001, 1], step: 0.001 }
      curve f = x^2 { where: x >= 0, color: neutral, width: 3 }
      point a = (1, 1)
      point b = (1 + h, (1 + h)^2) { color: distance }
      line sec { through: a, slope: ((1 + h)^2 - 1)/h, color: speed, width: 3 }
      label at (0.05, 5.2) = "slope of the line = ${fixed(((1 + h)^2 - 1)/h, 2)}" { color: speed }
      slider h { label: "gap" }
    }

    goal "Shrink the [gap]{gap} until the line just touches the curve" {
      when: h < 0.05
      hints: ["Watch what the line does as the second point slides in.", "Keep going until the two points look like one."]
      showme: { h: 0.01 }
    }

    quiz {
      ask "The slope of that touching line is the same as..."
      skill: "derivative-meaning"
      * "the car's speed at $t = 1$"
      - "the distance at $t = 1$" { why: "The distance at $t = 1$ is 1 m, the height of the point. The slope is how steep the line is, not how high." }
      - "the average over the first second" { why: "That was the line at a gap of 1, with slope 3. You shrank it until it stopped being an average over anything." }
      after: goals
    }
  }

  slide "What mathematicians write" {
    id: "d-name"
    beat: name
    > Everything you just did has a name. Call the car's distance $f$. The [gap]{gap} is $h$. The [average]{speed} is a fraction. Heading for is $\lim$. And the number it settles on is the **derivative**.

    scene plane {
      x: [0, 10]
      y: [0, 6]
      axes: false
      param h = 0.01 { range: [0.001, 1], step: 0.001, keep }
      param m = 0 { range: [0, 1] }
      label w1 at (0.5, 5) = "gap = ${h}" { color: gap, size: 20 }
      label s1 at (0.5, 5) = "h = ${h}" { color: gap, size: 20 }
      label w2 at (0.5, 3.4) = "average speed = ${fixed(((1 + h)^2 - 1)/h, 3)}" { color: speed, size: 20 }
      label s2 at (0.5, 3.4) = "\dfrac{\textcolor{distance}{f(1 + h) - f(1)}}{\textcolor{gap}{h}} = ${fixed(((1 + h)^2 - 1)/h, 3)}" { tex, size: 20 }
      label w3 at (0.5, 1.4) = "settles on 2" { color: speed, size: 20 }
      label s3 at (0.5, 1.4) = "\lim_{\textcolor{gap}{h} \to 0} \dfrac{\textcolor{distance}{f(1 + h) - f(1)}}{\textcolor{gap}{h}} = f'(1) = 2" { tex, size: 20 }
      morph w1 -> s1 { by: m }
      morph w2 -> s2 { by: m }
      morph w3 -> s3 { by: m }
      step "In words, the way you've been saying it." { set: { m: 0 } }
      step "In symbols, the way it gets written." { animate: { m: 1 }, dur: 1600 }
      slider h { label: "gap" }
    }

    build {
      ask "Put the definition together."
      skill: "derivative-from-definition"
      template: "$f'(1) =$ ___ ___"
      bank: ["$\lim_{h \to 0}$", "$\lim_{h \to 1}$", "$\dfrac{f(1 + h) - f(1)}{h}$", "$\dfrac{f(h) - f(1)}{h}$", "$\big(f(1 + h) - f(1)\big) \cdot h$"]
      answer: ["$\lim_{h \to 0}$", "$\dfrac{f(1 + h) - f(1)}{h}$"]
      hint "lim means where it's heading, the same idea as in the limits lessons."
      hint "The gap is heading for 0, and the average is the change in distance divided by the gap."
      after: 2
      ! "The gap is $h$, heading for 0. The change in distance over the gap is the average. Where it settles is $f'(1)$."
    }
  }

  slide "A corner" {
    id: "d-corner"
    beat: breaker
    > Does every curve have a slope at every point? Try this one at 0.

    scene plane {
      x: [-2, 2]
      y: [-0.4, 2]
      aspect: equal
      grid
      axes
      param hr = 1 { range: [0.001, 1], step: 0.001 }
      param hl = 1 { range: [0.001, 1], step: 0.001 }
      curve f = abs(x) { color: neutral, width: 3 }
      point o = (0, 0)
      point r = (hr, hr) { color: gap }
      point l = (0 - hl, hl) { color: distance }
      line right = (0, 0) -> (hr, hr) { color: gap, width: 3 }
      line left = (0 - hl, hl) -> (0, 0) { color: distance, width: 3 }
      label at (0.15, 1.85) = "slope on the right = ${fixed(abs(hr)/hr, 2)}" { color: gap }
      label at (-1.95, 1.85) = "slope on the left = ${fixed((0 - abs(0 - hl))/hl, 2)}" { color: distance }
      slider hr { label: "gap on the right" }
      slider hl { label: "gap on the left" }
    }

    goal "Shrink both gaps below 0.01" {
      when: hr < 0.01 and hl < 0.01
      hints: ["There are two sliders this time, one for each side.", "Watch the two readouts as the gaps shrink. Do they meet?"]
      showme: { hr: 0.005, hl: 0.005 }
    }

    quiz {
      ask "So does $y = |x|$ have a slope at 0?"
      skill: "derivative-meaning"
      * "No: the right settles on 1 and the left on $-1$"
      - "Yes, it's 0, halfway between" { why: "Neither side ever gets near 0. Averaging two different answers doesn't make a single one." }
      - "Yes, it's 1" { why: "That's only the right side. From the left the line slopes down at $-1$." }
      after: goals
      ! "No single number to settle on, so no derivative. A sharp corner breaks it."
    }
  }

  slide "A jump" {
    id: "d-jump"
    beat: breaker
    > One more. What happens at a jump?

    scene plane {
      x: [0, 4]
      y: [-0.5, 4.5]
      grid
      axes
      param h = 1 { range: [0.001, 1], step: 0.001 }
      curve lower = 1 { where: x <= 2, color: neutral, width: 3 }
      curve upper = 3 { where: x > 2, color: neutral, width: 3 }
      point a = (2, 1)
      point hole = (2, 3) { open }
      point b = (2 + h, 3) { color: distance }
      line sec = (2, 1) -> (2 + h, 3) { color: speed, width: 3 }
      label at (0.1, 4.2) = "average over the gap = ${fixed(2/h, 1)}" { color: speed }
      slider h { label: "gap" }
    }

    goal "Shrink the gap below 0.01" {
      when: h < 0.01
      hints: ["Drag the gap slider left and keep an eye on the readout."]
      showme: { h: 0.005 }
    }

    quiz {
      ask "As the gap shrinks at $x = 2$, the average..."
      skill: "derivative-meaning"
      * "keeps growing, it never settles"
      - "settles on 2" { why: "The jump stays 2 tall however small the gap gets, so it's 2 divided by something shrinking. That grows without end." }
      - "settles on 0" { why: "The gap goes to 0, but it's on the bottom of the fraction. A smaller bottom makes the answer bigger." }
      after: goals
      ! "Corners and jumps are where derivatives go missing."
    }
  }

  slide "Without the slider" {
    id: "d-worked"
    beat: example
    > Here's how you get $f'(1)$ without a slider, just algebra. Step through it.

    scene plane {
      x: [0, 10]
      y: [0, 6]
      axes: false
      param s = 0 { range: [0, 4], step: 1 }
      label at (0.4, 5) = "f(1 + h) - f(1) = (1 + h)^2 - 1" { tex, size: 20, show: s >= 1 }
      label at (0.4, 3.8) = "= 2h + h^2" { tex, size: 20, show: s >= 2 }
      label at (0.4, 2.6) = "\text{divide by } h\!: \quad 2 + h" { tex, size: 20, show: s >= 3 }
      label at (0.4, 1.4) = "\text{as } h \to 0\!: \quad 2 + h \to 2" { tex, size: 20, show: s >= 4 }
      step "Start with the change in distance." { set: { s: 1 } }
      step "Multiply out the bracket." { set: { s: 2 } }
      step "Divide by the gap." { set: { s: 3 } }
      step "Let the gap head for 0." { set: { s: 4 } }
    }

    quiz {
      ask "Why are we allowed to divide by $h$ here?"
      skill: "derivative-from-definition"
      * "Because $h$ isn't 0, it's only heading there"
      - "Because $h$ is 0" { why: "If $h$ were 0 we'd be dividing by 0, the trap from before." }
      - "Because dividing by 0 is fine in calculus" { why: "It never is. That's the whole reason for the limit." }
      after: 3
    }
  }

  slide "You finish it" {
    id: "d-faded"
    beat: example
    > Same car, now at $t = 3$. We've done the algebra. You finish it.
    >
    > $$\frac{f(3 + h) - f(3)}{h} = \frac{(3 + h)^2 - 9}{h} = \frac{6h + h^2}{h}$$

    numeric {
      ask "What's $f'(3)$?"
      skill: "derivative-from-definition"
      unit: "m/s"
      answer: 6
      expect: 2 * 3
      wrong 7 "That's the average with a gap of 1, a whole second." -> "d-detour-zero" retry
      wrong 9 "9 m is where the car is at $t = 3$." -> "d-detour-value" retry
      hint "Divide the top by $h$ first. Then let $h$ head for 0."
    }
  }

  slide "Your turn" {
    id: "d-own-1"
    beat: example
    > Your turn, three of them. The letter changes to $x$. Nothing else does.

    numeric {
      ask "$f(x) = x^2$. What's $f'(5)$?"
      skill: "derivative-from-definition"
      answer: 10
      expect: 2 * 5
      wrong 25 "25 is $f(5)$, the height. The derivative is how steep." -> "d-detour-value" retry
      hint "Work out $\dfrac{f(5 + h) - f(5)}{h}$, tidy it up, then let $h$ head for 0."
    }
  }

  slide "Two terms" {
    id: "d-own-2"
    beat: example
    > A new function this time.

    numeric {
      ask "$f(x) = x^2 + 3x$. What's $f'(1)$?"
      skill: "derivative-from-definition"
      answer: 5
      expect: 2 * 1 + 3
      wrong 4 "4 is $f(1)$, the height. The derivative is how steep." -> "d-detour-value" retry
      wrong 2 "That's only the $x^2$ part. The $3x$ changes too."
      hint "$f(1 + h) - f(1)$ has an $x^2$ part and a $3x$ part. Do both, then divide by $h$."
    }
  }

  slide "Going downhill" {
    id: "d-own-3"
    beat: example
    > Last one.

    numeric {
      ask "$f(x) = x^2$. What's $f'(-1)$?"
      skill: "derivative-from-definition"
      answer: -2
      expect: 2 * (0 - 1)
      wrong 2 "Right size. Check the direction: is the curve going up or down at $x = -1$?" -> "d-detour-sign" retry
      wrong 1 "1 is $f(-1)$, the height. The derivative is how steep." -> "d-detour-value" retry
      hint "$(-1 + h)^2 - 1$ multiplies out to $-2h + h^2$."
    }
  }

  slide "A falling ball" {
    id: "d-transfer"
    beat: transfer
    > A ball is dropped. After $t$ seconds its height is $20 - 5t^2$ metres.

    numeric {
      ask "At $t = 1$, what's the rate of change of its height?"
      skill: "derivative-from-definition"
      unit: "m/s"
      answer: -10
      expect: 0 - 10 * 1
      wrong 10 "Right size, but the ball is falling, so its height is going down." -> "d-detour-sign" retry
      wrong 15 "15 m is its height at $t = 1$, where it is, not how fast that's changing." -> "d-detour-value" retry
      wrong -5 "That's the average over the whole first second. Shrink the gap." -> "d-detour-big-gap" retry
      ! "That's the derivative. It'll come back in practice over the next few days, mixed in with other things."
    }
  }

  slide "Where it is, not how far it went" {
    id: "d-detour-distance"
    beat: detour
    hidden: true
    > Easy slip: that's where the car *is*, not how far it went. In that second it went from 1 m to 4 m.

    numeric {
      ask "Try another second. Average speed between $t = 2$ and $t = 3$?"
      skill: "derivative-meaning"
      unit: "m/s"
      answer: 5
      expect: (3^2 - 2^2) / (3 - 2)
      wrong 9 "9 m is where it is at $t = 3$. How far did it go that second?"
      hint "It's at 4 m when $t = 2$ and 9 m when $t = 3$."
    }
  }

  slide "A gap that's too big" {
    id: "d-detour-big-gap"
    beat: detour
    hidden: true
    > That's an average over a big gap, and the car kept speeding up the whole time. The shorter the [gap]{gap}, the closer you get to what it's doing right now.

    scene plane {
      x: [-0.2, 2.4]
      y: [-0.5, 5.6]
      grid
      axes
      param h = 1 { range: [0.001, 1], step: 0.001 }
      curve f = x^2 { where: x >= 0, color: neutral, width: 3 }
      line rise = (1 + h, 1) -> (1 + h, (1 + h)^2) { color: distance, width: 4 }
      point a = (1, 1)
      point b = (1 + h, (1 + h)^2) { color: distance }
      label at (0.05, 5.2) = "gap = ${h} s" { color: gap }
      label at (0.05, 4.7) = "average speed = ${fixed(((1 + h)^2 - 1)/h, 3)} m/s" { color: speed }
      slider h { label: "gap" }
    }

    goal "Shrink the [gap]{gap} below 0.005 and watch the last digits" {
      when: h < 0.005
      hints: ["Drag the gap slider right down to the left end."]
      showme: { h: 0.001 }
      dur: 3000
    }
  }

  slide "Nearly there" {
    id: "d-detour-zero"
    beat: detour
    hidden: true
    > You're nearly there. After dividing you have something with $h$ still in it, and $h$ isn't 1. It's heading for 0.

    numeric {
      ask "As $h$ heads for 0, where does $\dfrac{8h + h^2}{h}$ head?"
      skill: "derivative-from-definition"
      answer: 8
      wrong 9 "That's with $h = 1$. Divide by $h$ first, then make $h$ tiny: 0.1, 0.01, 0.001."
    }
  }

  slide "Where it is versus how fast" {
    id: "d-detour-value"
    beat: detour
    hidden: true
    > That's the [height]{distance}, where it is. The derivative is [how steep]{speed}, how fast it's changing. Slide the point and compare them.

    scene plane {
      x: [-3, 3]
      y: [-1, 9]
      grid
      axes
      param a = 1 { range: [-2.5, 2.5], step: 0.5 }
      curve f = x^2 { color: neutral, width: 3 }
      point p = (a, a^2) { drag: x -> a, snap: 0.5, color: distance }
      line tan { through: p, slope: 2*a, color: speed, width: 3, style: dashed }
      line up = (a, 0) -> (a, a^2) { color: distance, width: 3 }
      label at (-2.9, 8.5) = "height = ${a^2}" { color: distance }
      label at (-2.9, 7.7) = "steepness = ${2*a}" { color: speed }
    }

    goal "Find a spot where the height and the steepness are the same number" {
      when: a^2 == 2*a
      hints: ["They're different almost everywhere. Try whole numbers of $x$.", "Try $x = 2$: the height is 4. How steep is it there?"]
      showme: { a: 2 }
    }

    numeric {
      ask "$f(x) = x^2$. How steep is it at $x = 4$?"
      skill: "derivative-from-definition"
      answer: 8
      expect: 2 * 4
      wrong 16 "16 is the height at $x = 4$. How steep is it there?"
      after: goals
    }
  }

  slide "Going down" {
    id: "d-detour-sign"
    beat: detour
    hidden: true
    > Right size, wrong direction. Where the curve goes down, the slope is negative.

    scene plane {
      x: [-3, 3]
      y: [-1, 9]
      grid
      axes
      param a = -1.5 { range: [-2.5, 2.5], step: 0.5 }
      curve f = x^2 { color: neutral, width: 3 }
      point p = (a, a^2) { drag: x -> a, snap: 0.5, color: distance }
      line tan { through: p, slope: 2*a, color: speed, width: 3, style: dashed }
      label at (-2.9, 8.5) = "slope = ${2*a}" { color: speed }
    }

    goal "Find a spot where the slope is positive" {
      when: a > 0
      hints: ["Positive means the line climbs as you go right."]
    }

    numeric {
      ask "$f(x) = x^2$. What's $f'(-3)$?"
      skill: "derivative-from-definition"
      answer: -6
      expect: 2 * (0 - 3)
      wrong 6 "Right size. Which way does the curve go at $x = -3$?"
      after: goals
    }
  }
}
